AWK(1)                         P32W Unix                          AWK(1)

NAME
       awk - pattern scanning and processing language

SYNOPSIS
       awk [-F fs] [-v var=value] ... 'program' [var=value | file] ...
       awk [-F fs] [-v var=value] ... -f progfile ... [var=value | file]
           ...

DESCRIPTION
       awk reads its input a record (normally a line) at a time,
       splits each record into fields, and runs the program on it.
       This is POSIX awk, the language of "The AWK Programming
       Language" (Aho, Kernighan and Weinberger, 1988).

       The program is a list of pattern-action pairs:

              pattern { action }

       For each record, every pattern is tried in order, and the
       action of each that matches is run. A missing pattern matches
       every record; a missing action prints the record. BEGIN
       actions run before any input is read, END actions after the
       last record (where $0 and NF still hold it). A program with
       only BEGIN actions reads no input.

       The input is each file operand in turn, or the standard input
       when there are none (or for a file named -). An operand of
       the form var=value assigns the variable when awk reaches it
       in the list, between files.

OPTIONS
       -F fs  the field separator FS (-F t and -F '\t' are a tab)
       -v var=value
              assign var before the BEGIN actions run (\n, \t and the
              like in value are escapes, as in a string)
       -f progfile
              read the program from progfile (- is the standard
              input); several -f are joined in order
       --     the end of the options
       -version
              print awk's version

PATTERNS
       BEGIN, END
              (above); there may be several of each
       expression
              matches when it is true: a nonzero number or a
              non-empty string (a field that looks numeric counts as
              a number: $1 is false when it is 0)
       /regex/
              matches when $0 matches regex
       pattern1, pattern2
              a range: from a record that matches pattern1 through
              the next that matches pattern2 (the same record may do
              both)

ACTIONS
       Statements are separated by newlines or semicolons; a
       backslash at the end of a line joins it to the next, and a
       newline after { && || , do else is ignored. # starts a
       comment.

              expression
              print [expression-list] [> file]
              printf format [, expression-list] [> file]
              if (expression) statement [else statement]
              while (expression) statement
              do statement while (expression)
              for (expr; expr; expr) statement
              for (var in array) statement
              { statements }
              break   continue
              next             start on the next record
              nextfile         start on the next file
              exit [expression]
                               run the END actions (from END: stop),
                               then exit with the value (0)
              return [expression]
              delete array[subscript]   delete array
              getline ...      (see INPUT AND OUTPUT)

EXPRESSIONS
       Values are strings or numbers, converted as needed. Constants
       are numbers (12, 1.5, 3e10) and strings ("a\tb"; the escapes
       are \n \t \r \b \f \v \a \\ \" \/ and \ddd in octal). A
       variable needs no declaring: never set, it is both "" and 0.

       The operators, from the loosest:

              = += -= *= /= %= ^=   assignment, right to left
              ?:                    a ? b : c, right to left
              ||  &&                or, and (only what is needed
                                    is evaluated)
              in                    (k in array), ((i,j) in array)
              ~ !~                  matches, does not match
              < <= == != > >=       comparison
              (none)                concatenation: a b
              + -
              * / %                 % keeps the sign of the left
              ! - +                 not, unary minus and plus
              ^ **                  power, right to left
              ++ --                 before or after a variable
              $                     a field: $1, $NF, $(i+1)
              ( )                   grouping

       A comparison is numeric when both sides are numbers or
       strings from the input that look like numbers (fields,
       getline, FS-split elements, ARGV, ENVIRON, -v values); it is
       a string comparison otherwise. Write "" after a value to make
       it a string (x ""), add 0 to make it a number (x + 0). In a
       print list, put a comparison with > in parentheses.

       Numbers are printed as integers when they are whole (up to 30
       digits, then by %.30g), and otherwise by OFMT (print) or
       CONVFMT (other conversions to a string).

VARIABLES
       NF        the number of fields; setting it cuts $0 short or
                 adds empty fields
       NR, FNR   the record number, in all and in this file
       FS        the field separator (" ")
       OFS, ORS  print's field and record separators (" ", "\n")
       RS        the record separator ("\n"); "" makes paragraphs
                 (records end at blank lines, and newline separates
                 fields too); longer than one character, it is a
                 regular expression
       FILENAME  the input file's name ("" for the standard input)
       SUBSEP    joins subscripts: a[i,j] is a[i SUBSEP j] ("\034")
       RSTART, RLENGTH
                 set by match()
       CONVFMT, OFMT
                 number to string formats ("%.6g")
       ENVIRON   the environment: ENVIRON["HOME"]
       ARGC, ARGV
                 the operands: ARGV[0] is awk, ARGV[1] to
                 ARGV[ARGC-1] the rest (they may be changed in
                 BEGIN: an empty one is skipped)

       Fields: FS " " splits at runs of blanks and newlines, leading
       and trailing ones ignored; any other single character splits
       at each one; "" splits into characters; a longer FS is a
       regular expression. Assigning a field (even $(NF+2)) or NF
       makes $0 again from the fields with OFS; assigning $0 splits
       it again. A change to FS applies from the next record.

ARRAYS
       Arrays are associative: subscripts are strings (a number is
       converted by CONVFMT, a whole one as an integer). An element
       is made when it is referred to; (k in a) tests without
       making it. for (k in a) visits the subscripts in no
       particular order. length(a) is the number of elements.

FUNCTIONS
              function name(params) { statements }

       (func is the same.) Scalars are passed by value, arrays by
       reference; an argument never set may become an array in the
       function. Parameters not passed are local variables, so the
       convention is to list locals as extra parameters, after some
       blanks. A call needs no blank before the (. Recursion works.

       Built-in functions:

       length[(s)]   the length of s ($0); of an array, its size
       substr(s, m[, n])
                     at most n characters of s from position m (from
                     1; m and n are rounded)
       index(s, t)   where t first appears in s, or 0
       split(s, a[, fs])
                     split s into a[1], a[2] ... by fs (FS) or a
                     regex; the number of elements
       sub(re, repl[, t])
                     replace the first match of re in t ($0) by repl,
                     in which & is the matched text, \\& a literal &
                     (written "\\&"); the number replaced (0 or 1)
       gsub(re, repl[, t])
                     the same for every match (an empty match next to
                     the previous one is not replaced)
       match(s, re)  where re first matches in s (the longest
                     match there), or 0; sets RSTART and RLENGTH
                     (-1 if none)
       sprintf(fmt, ...)
                     the string printf would print
       tolower(s), toupper(s)
       int(x)        x with the fraction cut off
       sqrt(x), exp(x), log(x), sin(x), cos(x), atan2(y, x)
       rand()        a random number in [0, 1)
       srand([x])    start rand anew from x (the time of day); the
                     previous seed
       system(cmd)   run cmd with sh; its exit status
       close(name)   close a file or pipe; for a pipe, the command's
                     exit status; -1 if it was not open
       fflush([name])
                     write out a file's output (all files)

       A regular expression argument may be a /regex/ or a string.

INPUT AND OUTPUT
       print writes its expressions separated by OFS and followed by
       ORS (print alone writes $0); printf writes them by its format:
       %d %i %o %u %x %X %c %s %e %E %f %F %g %G and %%, with the
       flags - + space # 0, a width and a .precision (* takes either
       from the arguments). %c of a number is the character with that
       code. Output goes to the standard output, or:

              > file      the file, emptied when first opened
              >> file     the file, added to
              | command   the input of command (run by sh)

       /dev/stdout and /dev/stderr are understood. A file or pipe
       stays open, so later prints go on adding to it, until it is
       closed; awk waits for its commands at the end.

       getline reads a record:

              getline              $0 NF NR FNR from the input
              getline var          var NR FNR from the input
              getline < file       $0 NF from file
              getline var < file   var from file
              cmd | getline        $0 NF NR from command cmd
              cmd | getline var    var NR from command cmd

       It returns 1, 0 at the end of the input, or -1 if the file
       cannot be opened: write while ((getline line < f) > 0).

NUMBERS
       awk's numbers are IEEE doubles (about 16 digits), but P32W has
       no floating point: a whole number that fits in 32 bits is
       kept as an integer, and the rest go through the software
       floating point of the C library. So counting and adding up
       whole numbers is fast, and fractions are slower but give the
       same results as on any IEEE machine. Number output is exact
       (printf "%.20f" shows the double's true digits).

REGULAR EXPRESSIONS
       POSIX extended regular expressions:

              c         a character matches itself
              .         any character
              [...]     a character in the set (ranges a-z, classes
                        [:alpha:] [:digit:] [:alnum:] [:upper:]
                        [:lower:] [:space:] [:blank:] [:punct:]
                        [:print:] [:graph:] [:cntrl:] [:xdigit:])
              [^...]    a character not in the set
              ^ $       the beginning, the end of the string
              r*  r+  r?
                        zero or more, one or more, zero or one r
              r{m,n}    m to n r's ({m}, {m,})
              r1|r2     either
              (r)       grouping
              \c        c itself; \n \t \/ \" \ddd and the like are
                        escapes, as in strings

       The match is the leftmost, and of those the longest. In a
       string used as a regular expression the backslashes must be
       doubled ("\\." is a literal dot).

EXAMPLES
       awk '{ s += $3 } END { print s }' file      sum column 3
       awk 'NF > 3' file           lines with more than 3 fields
       awk -F: '{ print $1 }' /etc/passwd          the user names
       awk '!seen[$0]++'           drop repeated lines
       awk '{ n[$1]++ } END { for (w in n) print w, n[w] }'
                                   count the different first words
       awk 'length > 72 { print FILENAME ": " FNR }' *.c
       awk '{ $2 = ""; print }'    without the second field
       awk 'BEGIN { printf "%.3f\n", atan2(0, -1) }'          pi

EXIT STATUS
       0; the value given to exit; 2 for a syntax error in the
       program or an error while it runs (division by zero, a file
       that cannot be opened for input...), which awk reports with
       the source line (and the input record) where it happened.

SEE ALSO
       sed(1), grep(1), sh(1), bc(1), printf(3), "The AWK
       Programming Language" by Aho, Kernighan and Weinberger

BUGS
       Differences from the one-true-awk (macOS's awk), on purpose:
       substr rounds its arguments as POSIX says (substr("hello", 0,
       2) is "h"); cmd | getline adds to NR, as POSIX says; close
       of a pipe returns the command's exit status; strings like
       0x1A are not hexadecimal numbers (they are 0); %u %x %o of a
       negative number print its 64-bit two's complement; %c of 0
       writes a NUL; a comparison may stand unparenthesized in a
       print list (but > there redirects). The order of for (k in
       a) is not the same as other awks'.

       Input numbers with more than 17 significant digits, or very
       large or small exponents, are converted to the nearest
       double within an ulp or two (softfp's conversion); the output
       is exact. %d of a number beyond 64 bits is saturated. At most
       16 files and pipes are open at once. The program and its
       data must fit in about 3.5 MB: an array holds some 40,000
       short elements. Recursion stops (with an error) before the
       1 MiB stack runs out: about 2,500 calls deep. rand gives a
       different sequence than other awks for the same seed.
